[Thinking]
**Creating a simple TCP server**

I need to create a simple TCP server in C, possibly following POSIX standards. I’m considering whether I should ask about the user's specific operating system, but I could provide a Linux example to keep things straightforward. Let's craft a concise explanation along with code and clear compile instructions for the user. Making sure it’s user-friendly will be important, so I want to keep it as simple as possible.
[/Thinking]

Here’s a simple **TCP/IP server in C** for **Linux/Unix (POSIX)**. It listens on a port, accepts one client at a time, receives a message, and replies back.

### TCP Server in C
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>

#define PORT 8080
#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

int main() {
    int server_fd, client_fd;
    struct sockaddr_in server_addr, client_addr;
    socklen_t client_len = sizeof(client_addr);
    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
    ssize_t bytes_read;

    // Create socket
    server_fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    if (server_fd < 0) {
        perror("socket failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Allow address reuse
    int opt = 1;
    if (setsockopt(server_fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEADDR, &opt, sizeof(opt)) < 0) {
        perror("setsockopt failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Setup server address
    memset(&server_addr, 0, sizeof(server_addr));
    server_addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    server_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;   // Accept connections on any IP
    server_addr.sin_port = htons(PORT);

    // Bind socket
    if (bind(server_fd, (struct sockaddr *)&server_addr, sizeof(server_addr)) < 0) {
        perror("bind failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Listen for connections
    if (listen(server_fd, 5) < 0) {
        perror("listen failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    printf("Server listening on port %d...\n", PORT);

    // Accept one client connection
    client_fd = accept(server_fd, (struct sockaddr *)&client_addr, &client_len);
    if (client_fd < 0) {
        perror("accept failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    printf("Client connected: %s:%d\n",
           inet_ntoa(client_addr.sin_addr),
           ntohs(client_addr.sin_port));

    // Receive data from client
    bytes_read = read(client_fd, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE - 1);
    if (bytes_read < 0) {
        perror("read failed");
    } else {
        buffer[bytes_read] = '\0';
        printf("Client says: %s\n", buffer);

        // Send response
        const char *reply = "Hello from server!";
        send(client_fd, reply, strlen(reply), 0);
    }

    // Close sockets
    close(client_fd);
    close(server_fd);

    return 0;
}
```

---

### Compile and Run
```bash
gcc server.c -o server
./server
```

---

### Example Client Test
You can test it using `netcat`:
```bash
echo "Hi server" | nc 127.0.0.1 8080
```

---

If you want, I can also give you:
1. a **TCP client in C**, or  
2. a **multi-client server using threads**.

